Suppose an analysis runs correctly today. Six months later — during peer review, when a collaborator joins the project, or when you return to extend the work for a follow-up paper — the same code is checked out and run again.
The source code is byte-for-byte identical, yet the result can still change, or the code can fail to run at all, because:

a dependency has released a new major version with a changed or removed function;
a transitive dependency (one you never installed directly) has changed behaviour;
a package has dropped support for the Python version you are using;
a collaborator’s machine has a different Python version installed by default;
packages installed globally for an unrelated project are shadowing the ones this project expects.

None of these require you to have made a mistake. They are the ordinary, expected consequence of software evolving over time, combined with not having recorded which point in that evolution your result depended on.

Code is necessary, but not sufficient
For computational research, this leads to an important principle: your source code is not, by itself, the complete description of the computation you ran. The result of running analysis.py is a function of the code and of the interpreter and package versions available when it runs. Two people running identical code with different environments are not necessarily running the same computation.
A reproducible project therefore needs to record enough information about its environment that the environment itself can be reconstructed later — ideally automatically, and ideally by someone who was not involved in creating it.


Reproducibility is a spectrum, not a switch
A lockfile is a large improvement over “whatever happens to be installed,” but it does not make a project reproducible in an absolute sense. It fixes the Python-package layer of the environment; it says nothing about:

the operating system and its own system libraries;
hardware differences (for example, floating-point behaviour on different CPU architectures, or GPU driver versions);
external programs the code shells out to;
the data itself, if it is not version-controlled alongside the code;
random seeds, if stochastic algorithms are involved;
external services (APIs, databases) whose state can change independently of your code.

Some of these can be addressed with further tooling — containers (Docker, Apptainer) address the operating-system layer; explicit seeding addresses stochasticity; data version control tools (DVC, Git LFS) address large or changing datasets. The right amount of machinery depends on the stakes: a teaching demonstration needs less rigour than a clinical trial’s analysis pipeline.
The realistic goal for this session is narrower and more achievable: make the Python computational environment explicit, version-controlled, and mechanically reconstructable, rather than dependent on whatever happened to already be installed on one particular laptop. That alone removes one of the most common causes of “it works on my machine.”

A package manager is software that installs, updates, removes, and keeps track of other software packages, together with the constraints between them. Operating systems have long had their own (apt on Debian/Ubuntu, brew on macOS, winget on Windows); Python has its own ecosystem, built around the Python Package Index (PyPI) as the central public repository.
In Python, a package is simply reusable code, published so that other people do not need to write it themselves. For example:

pandas provides data structures and operations for tabular data;
numpy provides fast, array-based numerical computing;
scikit-learn provides general-purpose machine-learning algorithms;
jupyterlab provides an interactive, notebook-based interface to Python.


Dependencies, and why resolution is hard
A package can itself depend on other packages, which depend on others in turn. These indirect requirements are called transitive dependencies. Installing pandas, for example, pulls in numpy as a dependency without you asking for numpy by name.
This quickly produces a dependency graph rather than a simple list. Two direct dependencies you ask for might each place different, incompatible constraints on a shared transitive dependency — package A might require numpy&gt;=1.24, while package B only supports numpy&lt;1.24. Finding a single set of versions that satisfies every constraint simultaneously is known as dependency resolution, and in general it is a computationally hard problem (it is closely related to the Boolean satisfiability problem, SAT). This is precisely the part of environment management that is tedious and error-prone to do by hand, and precisely the part a package manager exists to automate.
Historically, Python’s own installer, pip, resolved dependencies using a comparatively simple, backtracking algorithm, and for a long time did not resolve them at all — it simply installed packages in the order given, sometimes silently overwriting an already-installed, compatible version with an incompatible one. This is one of the main reasons the community has since built more sophisticated tools (pip-tools, Poetry, PDM, conda, and now uv) around a proper resolver.


Why use uv?
uv, developed by Astral, is deliberately scoped to do more than resolve and install packages. It also:

installs and manages Python interpreters themselves (not just packages);
creates and manages per-project virtual environments automatically;
adds and removes a project’s declared dependencies;
resolves a consistent, compatible set of versions for the full dependency graph;
writes a lockfile recording exactly which versions were resolved;
runs commands inside the correct project environment on request.

Two practical reasons make uv a good default choice for a short teaching session and for everyday research use. First, it is implemented in Rust and is substantially faster than the pure-Python tools it replaces, particularly for dependency resolution and installation — for a live demonstration, this means we are not sitting waiting for a spinner. Second, it deliberately unifies steps that other tools historically required several separate programs for (interpreter installation, environment creation, dependency locking), which reduces the number of moving parts a beginner needs to hold in their head at once.






uv is not the only viable option



conda/mamba remain the standard choice when a project’s dependencies include non-Python components (compiled libraries, R packages, and so on) that PyPI does not distribute.
Poetry and PDM offer broadly similar project-based workflows to uv. The concepts in this session — declared dependencies, a resolved lockfile, and an isolated environment reconstructable from both — apply to all of them; only the specific commands differ.
